---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RemoveLineBreaks from '@/components/tools/text/RemoveLineBreaks.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="remove-line-breaks">
  <RemoveLineBreaks client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to remove line breaks from text</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste the text, usually copied from a PDF, an email or a terminal, or drop a file onto the box.</li>
      <li>Choose the action: unwrap paragraphs, remove every break, or only normalise the line endings.</li>
      <li>Pick the separator for joined lines: a space (the usual choice), nothing, a comma, a pipe or your own string.</li>
      <li>Leave trimming and space-collapsing on to clean up the uneven spacing that hard wrapping leaves.</li>
      <li>Copy the cleaned text or download it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Unwrap paragraphs, or remove every break?</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Unwrap paragraphs</strong> treats a blank line as a new paragraph and every other break as the
      place where the original column ended. It joins the lines in each block and keeps the blank line
      between blocks, so a three-paragraph letter stays three paragraphs. This is the right choice about 90%
      of the time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Remove every break</strong> puts the entire text on one line. Use it when you need a single
      value: a CSV cell, a JSON string, an SQL literal, or a column of values as a comma-separated list.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Normalise only</strong> keeps the structure. It converts Windows (CRLF) and classic Mac (CR)
      endings to Unix ones, limits runs of blank lines to one, and strips trailing spaces. Run it before you
      commit a file or compare two documents.
    </p>

    <h2>Why PDF text arrives broken</h2>
    <p>
      A PDF has no paragraphs. It stores glyphs at coordinates, so when you copy from one, the extractor
      guesses line breaks from vertical positions and puts a hard newline at the end of each visual line.
      In a document with a different column width, you get a stair-step of short lines. The same happens
      with plain-text emails wrapped at 72 characters, terminal output, and text copied from code editors
      with soft wrap off.
    </p>

    <h2>Line-ending reference</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th>Escape</th>
            <th>Bytes</th>
            <th>Used by</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Line feed (LF)</td><td><code>\n</code></td><td>0A</td><td>Unix, Linux, macOS, the web, Git</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Carriage return + line feed (CRLF)</td><td><code>\r\n</code></td><td>0D 0A</td><td>Windows, HTTP headers, SMTP, CSV per RFC 4180</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Carriage return (CR)</td><td><code>\r</code></td><td>0D</td><td>Classic Mac OS 9 and earlier</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Mixed endings in one file cause stray <code>^M</code> characters in an editor, a Git diff that shows
      every line as changed, and a CSV import that makes one giant row. Run “Normalise only” before you
      commit or import to fix all of these.
    </p>

    <h2>Common tasks</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>PDF or email to a document: unwrap paragraphs, join with a space, collapse repeated spaces.</li>
      <li>Column of values to a CSV row: remove every break, join with a comma.</li>
      <li>Text into a JSON or SQL string: remove every break, join with a space or with a literal <code>\n</code>.</li>
      <li>Cleaning a scraped page: normalise, then limit blank lines to one.</li>
      <li>Fixing a Windows file on Linux: normalise with LF endings selected.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Large files</h2>
    <p>
      The tool processes lines in chunks and lets the browser repaint between them, so a very long document
      does not freeze the tab. Your browser reads the file you drop, and the file stays on your device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
